# R-Type Delta: HD Boosted (PPSA26414) -- reach stage 1 gameplay.
#
# MEASURED end to end on 2026-08-20 (tools/screenshot, page cache evicted with tools/dropcache.py,
# unmodified binary, unmodified guest). The title's own module loads are the progress oracle -- the
# shell loads one PRX per scene from /app0/prx and PROSPER_MODLOG=1 names each one:
#
#   t~1    OPTIONS answers the title screen's own "PRESS <glyph>" prompt. The glyph is the PS5
#          OPTIONS icon (a filled vertical pill with three horizontal lines above it), read off
#          assets/screenshots/rtype-delta-title.png -- so that is the button the prompt NAMES. No
#          Cross-only arm was run, so whether the title would also accept Cross here is untested;
#          what is measured is that a route containing OPTIONS clears the title by t~12 s and a
#          no-input arm never clears it.
#   t~14   main menu. The title draws a one-line English description of the highlighted entry at the
#          bottom of the frame ("Start Game"), which is the state oracle this route was built against.
#   t~14-50  Cross walks Start Game -> Select Slot to Create -> Enter Name -> back to Start Game, and
#          writes <PROSPER_SAVE0>/PPSA26414/SaveData.dat. A FRESH save root has no pilot data, so the
#          title diverts the first "Start Game" into its pilot-creation flow; Cross alone completes it.
#   t~52   loadsel_Release.prx, then t~54 select_Release.prx -- the ship / Force-device select screen.
#   t~66   loads1_Release.prx, then t~74 st1r9_Release.prx -- stage 1 with the R-9.
#
# loadsel_Release.prx and loads1_Release.prx are the discriminator against attract mode: a matched
# NEUTRAL-INPUT control arm (neutral.pad, same command, same build) loaded title, select, st1r9, st2
# and st5 with no input at all -- the attract loop plays stage demos -- but never loadsel or loads1,
# and never wrote save data. So a stage PRX load on its own is NOT evidence of player-driven play;
# these two, plus SaveData.dat, are.
#
# Anchors are SECONDS from the FIRST PAD POLL (prosper/src/input/pad.cpp: a bare number is seconds,
# 'f' is flips, 'p' is pad reads). Windows are 0.3 s -- long enough that a 60 Hz poll cannot miss a
# press, short enough that a menu's auto-repeat cannot advance a selector twice.
#
# THE ROUTE IS SAVE-STATE DEPENDENT. Run it with a FRESH PROSPER_SAVE0 (#2734 made that a root, with
# saves namespaced <root>/PPSA26414/). With pilot data already present the title does not divert into
# the creation flow and the same button sequence lands somewhere else.
